window.MCHCC_BLOG_POSTS = [{"id":"6a68e426066bdd8b5a7d2424","title":"Together, We\u2019re Building a More Connected Future for Families","slug":"together-we-re-building-a-more-connected-future-for-families","excerpt":"CAPS & Maple City is officially operating as one organization, across five sites and a mobile unit, bringing together health care, family support, child abuse prevention education, and child advocacy services. By joining together, we can make it easier for children and families to access the care and support they need\u2014often without having to navigate multiple organizations or travel across the county. A family receiving support after a difficult experience can be connected to health care. A...","content":"CAPS & Maple City is officially operating as one organization, across five sites and a mobile unit, bringing together health care, family support, child abuse prevention education, and child advocacy services. By joining together, we can make it easier for children and families to access the care and support they need\u2014often without having to navigate multiple organizations or travel across the county. A family receiving support after a difficult experience can be connected to health care. A patient visiting for a medical appointment may also be introduced to family support or prevention services. Through our mobile clinic, we can bring care closer to where people live, learn, work, and gather. At the heart of this work is one shared belief: every child and family in our community deserves the opportunity to be healthy, safe, and thriving. Community Support Made This Possible We are deeply grateful to the donors, partners, volunteers, board members, staff, and community leaders who have supported this journey. The Community Foundation of Elkhart County provided a generous $500,000 grant to help offset initial merger-related expenses and support the early work of bringing our organizations together. This investment is helping build the foundation for a stronger and more coordinated system of comprehensive care. \u201cTogether, we are building something greater than the sum of its parts,\u201d said Rebecca Shetler Fast, CEO of CAPS & Maple City. \u201cWe are inspired by what lies ahead and remain committed to creating a seamless network of care that strengthens children, empowers families, and transforms lives across our community.\u201d Pete McCown, President of the Community Foundation of Elkhart County, shared, \u201cStrong communities are built when trusted organizations collaborate in ways that make services more accessible and effective. This merger reflects a commitment to stewardship, innovation, and long-term positive impact for families across our county.\u201d Looking Ahead\u2014Together The name CAPS & Maple City reflects both the trusted legacies that brought us here and the shared future we are building. As one organization, we remain deeply committed to the patients, clients, children, and families we serve. We are also committed to being thoughtful stewards of the resources our community entrusts to us. With the continued partnership of our supporters, we can reduce gaps in services, reach more families, and create a system of care that is more connected, more accessible, and easier to navigate. Thank you for believing in this work and for helping us build a healthier, safer community where children and families can thrive.","published":"2026-07-28T17:22:10Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_746c3ea259184a6992e5166cbf6c26b2~mv2.png/57b283_746c3ea259184a6992e5166cbf6c26b2~mv2.png#originWidth=2626&originHeight=1824","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"6a317ad2655fbab867101b3e","title":"Introducing Dr. Dwayne Johnson","slug":"introducing-dr-dwayne-johnson","excerpt":"Finding the right healthcare provider can be overwhelming. We understand that a first appointment with a new provider can leave patients feeling nervous, so we are eager to help make your first appointment feel familiar and welcoming by letting you get to know new providers early. We'd love to introduce you to Dr. Dwayne Johnson, the newest pediatrician joining our team! Dr. Johnson is a board-certified pediatrician with over 42 years of experience, dedicated to serving children and...","content":"Finding the right healthcare provider can be overwhelming. We understand that a first appointment with a new provider can leave patients feeling nervous, so we are eager to help make your first appointment feel familiar and welcoming by letting you get to know new providers early. We'd love to introduce you to Dr. Dwayne Johnson, the newest pediatrician joining our team! Dr. Johnson is a board-certified pediatrician with over 42 years of experience, dedicated to serving children and families\u2014primarily within the public health sector. Throughout his career, he has been deeply committed to the principle that every child deserves access to high-quality, compassionate healthcare, regardless of their background, circumstances, or ability to pay. From the beginning Dr. Johnson's professional journey began with four years of service in the military as a general physician, an experience that instilled in him a strong sense of duty, discipline, and service. Since then, he has devoted his career to caring for underserved and vulnerable populations, working to reduce health disparities and ensure that all children have the opportunity to thrive. \"I take great pride in delivering patient-centered care that not only addresses immediate medical needs but also supports long-term health, development, and well-being. I strongly believe in preventive care, family education, and community partnership as essential components of pediatric practice. Advocating for children\u2014especially those facing social, economic, or systemic barriers\u2014has been a central focus and driving force throughout my career.\" said Dr. Johnson Outside of the Office Happily married for over 30 years and a proud parent of three children, Dr. Johnson enjoys family time when outside of the pediatric office. His home is shared with a dog, a cat, and a conure, all of which bring joy and liveliness to his daily life. In his free time, he enjoys outdoor activities, American folk dancing, and traveling to new destinations. \"These experiences help me stay grounded, energized, and connected to the broader world\u2014perspectives I carry with me into my work caring for children and families,\" he shared. Welcome to the CAPS & Maple City team, Dr. Johnson! If you are looking for a new pediatrician or other provider, reach out to CAPS & Maple City! All insurances, including Medicaid & Medicare are welcome. Call us today at 574-534-3300 or visit our website to learn more about receiving care with us!","published":"2026-07-01T14:09:17Z","image":"wix:image://v1/927074_f35f0362a22341cc9d84d958f9c4fd56~mv2.png/927074_f35f0362a22341cc9d84d958f9c4fd56~mv2.png#originWidth=1080&originHeight=1350","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"6a2adf4c9095d1fb2e824aed","title":"A New Chapter for Children and Families","slug":"a-new-chapter-for-children-and-families","excerpt":"Families rarely experience challenges in isolation. A child's safety, a family's health needs, financial stress, barriers to access, and family stability are all connected. By bringing child abuse prevention, family support and parenting education alongside primary, dental, and behavioral health care together, CAPS & Maple City is building a more coordinated and compassionate system of care in our community. Together we now provide more than 15 unique services and supports, integrating...","content":"Families rarely experience challenges in isolation. A child's safety, a family's health needs, financial stress, barriers to access, and family stability are all connected. By bringing child abuse prevention, family support and parenting education alongside primary, dental, and behavioral health care together, CAPS & Maple City is building a more coordinated and compassionate system of care in our community. Together we now provide more than 15 unique services and supports, integrating clinical care and community services. Children and families thrive when they are safe, healthy, supported, and treated with dignity. This coordinated model of care is both practical and deeply personal. When a child and family comes to our doors after possible abuse or trauma, they are often facing one of the hardest moments of their lives. In that moment, every additional barrier matters. Having to travel to another city or navigate another system for medical care, behavioral health support, or follow-up services can add stress to an already overwhelming experience. As we work to integrate CAPS & Maple City, we are building a system where children and families can receive compassionate, trauma-informed care in one connected environment. A child may be able to receive a forensic interview, a medical evaluation, and connection to mental health support through the same coordinated team. A parent may be connected to health care, behavioral health, parenting support, or other resources without having to navigate multiple systems alone. Lizzy Diaz, SANE (Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner) Coordinator and a SANE Nurse, has shared \u201cHow we are meeting people where they are \u2014 how we choose to see them not just as a patient, but as a person who deserves dignity and respect, matters. We become not just a part of their medical journey, but a part of their healing.\u201d Lizzy Diaz, SANE Coordinator and Nurse, highlights the special care put into a SANE Exam Room. That is the heart of our work: seeing the whole person and the whole family. The challenges families face are often connected, and the support available to them should be connected too. By providing a suite of both clinical and community service programming, CAPS & Maple City can help close gaps, strengthen protective factors, and support families earlier and more holistically. Our vision remains clear: to help build a community where children are safer, families are stronger, and health and well-being are within reach. With the support of donors, volunteers, partners, and neighbors, CAPS & Maple City is creating a healthier, safer community where children and families can thrive.","published":"2026-06-11T16:44:05Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg#originWidth=6048&originHeight=4032","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"69df8aa147c3def1ffef9dd4","title":"It\u2019s Child Abuse Prevention Month. Here\u2019s What You Can Do (Beyond the Pinwheel)","slug":"beyondthepinwheel","excerpt":"Each April, you\u2019ll start to see blue and silver pinwheels appear across our community. They\u2019re bright. Hopeful. Easy to notice. And they matter. But planting a pinwheel is just the beginning. Because child abuse prevention doesn\u2019t happen from just planting pinwheels. It happens through everyday choices, everyday people, and a community that shows up, repeatedly. Here are a few simple, yet powerful ways you can be part of this work\u2014starting today. 1. Be a Safe Adult Every child deserves at...","content":"Each April, you\u2019ll start to see blue and silver pinwheels appear across our community. They\u2019re bright. Hopeful. Easy to notice. And they matter. But planting a pinwheel is just the beginning. Because child abuse prevention doesn\u2019t happen from just planting pinwheels. It happens through everyday choices, everyday people, and a community that shows up, repeatedly. Here are a few simple, yet powerful ways you can be part of this work\u2014starting today. 1. Be a Safe Adult Every child deserves at least one adult who makes them feel safe, seen, and heard. You don\u2019t need special training to start. You just need to be present. Listen without rushing Pay attention to changes in behavior Create space for kids to talk (even when it\u2019s messy or awkward) Sometimes prevention looks like a conversation that doesn\u2019t get ignored. 2. Support Families Before They Reach a Breaking Point Families don\u2019t need judgment. They need support. You can: Offer to help a friend who\u2019s overwhelmed Drop off a meal Watch a child so a parent can take a breath Simply check in and say, \u201cHey, how are you really doing?\u201d then listen Small moments of support can really make a difference in someone\u2019s life. 3. Learn the Signs (and Trust Your Gut) Prevention starts with awareness. Do you notice: Unexplained injuries Sudden behavioral changes Withdrawal or fearfulness In Indiana, everyone is required to report suspected abuse or neglect. You don\u2019t need proof, just a concern. 1-800-800-5556 (24/7) Speaking up doesn\u2019t accuse; it protects. And it could change everything. 4. Talk About It Child abuse prevention can feel like a heavy topic, but staying silent will never protect kids. Conversations do. Talk to your kids about boundaries and safety Share resources with friends or coworkers Use your voice to normalize prevention The more we talk about it, the less it stays hidden. 5. Show Up for Your Community Prevention isn\u2019t one program. It\u2019s a network of people who care. You can: Volunteer Attend local events Support organizations doing this work Share their message Every action helps build a community where children can grow up safe, healthy, and thriving.   6. Yes. Plant the Pinwheel Plant it in your yard. At your business. At your church. Let it be a visible reminder that you care and are making the promise to do what you can to keep kids safe. But don\u2019t stop there. Let it represent something deeper, a promise to showing up for kids in real ways every day. This Is How Prevention Works Not through one big moment. But through thousands of small ones. A conversation. A check-in. A helping hand. Someone that chooses, over and over again, to care. That\u2019s how children grow up safe. That\u2019s how families stay strong. That\u2019s how prevention works. Together.","published":"2026-04-15T13:05:00Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_cc24cd8cd1a54fe1babe3ed404ab5e88~mv2.jpg/57b283_cc24cd8cd1a54fe1babe3ed404ab5e88~mv2.jpg#originWidth=800&originHeight=538","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"69b43f290d1c152c6e6d3f9f","title":"CAPS and Maple City Announce Merger to Better Serve Families","slug":"caps-and-maple-city-health-care-center-announce-merger-to-better-serve-families","excerpt":"The boards of Child and Parent Services (CAPS) and Maple City Health Care Center (MCHCC)  each unanimously approved a merger on March 13th to strengthen and coordinate services for the families and communities they serve. The merger is expected to be completed by mid-year 2026 pending necessary regulatory approvals and notifications. CAPS and Maple City\u2019s missions closely align around one central belief: every family in the community deserves to be healthy, safe, and thriving. By merging, the...","content":"The boards of Child and Parent Services (CAPS) and Maple City Health Care Center (MCHCC)  each unanimously approved a merger on March 13th to strengthen and coordinate services for the families and communities they serve. The merger is expected to be completed by mid-year 2026 pending necessary regulatory approvals and notifications. CAPS and Maple City\u2019s missions closely align around one central belief: every family in the community deserves to be healthy, safe, and thriving. By merging, the boards aim to significantly reduce gaps in services, broaden reach, and strengthen long-term sustainability to create positive outcomes for families. \u201cThis merger will strengthen our resources and expand how we serve our community with more coordinated and complete care for families,\u201d said Rebecca Shetler Fast, incoming Chief Executive Officer of the merged organization and current CAPS CEO and President. \u201cOur shared goal is to create a healthier, safer community for children, families, and the communities we serve. Together we have the opportunity to dramatically increase our reach and our impact.\u201d Pete McCown, President of the Community Foundation of Elkhart County, added, \u201cStrong communities are built when trusted organizations collaborate in ways that make services more accessible and effective. This merger reflects a commitment to stewardship, innovation, and long-term positive impact for families across our county.\u201d Dr. Max Mertz, a current and founding board member of Maple City, shared, \"The vision has always been to improve our community\u2019s health by caring for the whole person through quality, affordable, integrated care. This merger brings together two organizations that support the health, safety, and stability of families, making it easier for everyone in our community to access the care and support they need.\" Paul Shetler Fast of Maple City will serve as Executive Director and Chief Operating Officer of the merged entity. Both current board chairs will serve as co-chairs of the new combined organization to ensure continuity during the transition. The organization\u2019s new operating name will be CAPS & Maple City. The transition is expected to be seamless with no disruption to services for families, clients, or patients. If you have questions, feel free to check out our CAPS & Maple City FAQs here [FAQs]","published":"2026-03-17T11:21:07Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_30123897f4d54e09ab833820a9c21dc1~mv2.png/57b283_30123897f4d54e09ab833820a9c21dc1~mv2.png#originWidth=2625&originHeight=1824","featured":true,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"69937f0e7e0fff32961228c5","title":"In the Moments That Matter Most\u00a0","slug":"in-the-moments-that-matter-most","excerpt":"Lizzy the Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner (SANE). When a child discloses sexual abuse, time feels different. The world keeps moving, but inside that space, everything shifts. Some move quickly. Others remain frozen, unsure what to do. Children ask quiet questions.   In those first hours, the response matters. Answers are needed. A few years ago, a young survivor in our community bravely reported abuse. Their family wanted to do everything right to seek care, to protect their child, and to move...","content":"Lizzy the Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner (SANE). When a child discloses sexual abuse, time feels different. The world keeps moving, but inside that space, everything shifts. Some move quickly. Others remain frozen, unsure what to do. Children ask quiet questions.   In those first hours, the response matters. Answers are needed. A few years ago, a young survivor in our community bravely reported abuse. Their family wanted to do everything right to seek care, to protect their child, and to move forward. But at that time, there was no access to a pediatric Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner (SANE) in Elkhart County. The only option for a specialized exam was to travel outside the county. That was not the news the family expected. It meant more time. More stress. More uncertainty. In the end, they decided not to pursue the medical forensic exam. And that moment reiterated something important; local access to pediatric SANEs can help ensure children receive specialized care in moments that matter most. A SANE is a specially trained nurse who provides medical care after a sexual assault or in cases of suspected sexual abuse. SANEs are trauma-informed, meaning they understand how trauma can shape the way a child thinks, feels, or responds. They move slowly. Gently. At a child\u2019s pace. A medical forensic exam is not only about evidence. It is about health, reassurance, and well-being. Despite how the words \u201cforensic exam\u201d may sound, the exam is not meant to be scary. Nothing is rushed. Nothing is forced. A child may choose to have a safe adult in the room or choose privacy. The priority is always the child\u2019s health and safety. Even if evidence cannot be collected, a trauma-informed SANE can offer reassurance, provide treatment, and connect families to support. This care reduces additional trauma, strengthens coordination between medical providers and investigators, and ensures children are met by professionals trained particularly for this moment. And it is important to say this clearly: Even when time has passed, an exam can still matter. Children often disclose abuse days, weeks, and even months later. Delayed disclosure is common. Fear, confusion, loyalty, or uncertainty can keep a child silent at first. For years, Elkhart County families did not have access to pediatric SANE services within the county, and neighboring partners worked together to support children and families in our county. The commitment has always been here. Now, access has expanded. Through CAPS\u2019 partnership with Maple City and collaboration across our community, pediatric SANE services are now available in Elkhart County. In the hardest moments, care is closer.","published":"2026-02-24T14:53:41Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_a4c4381b6a8e4ffe8d77208a14ed3837~mv2.jpg/57b283_a4c4381b6a8e4ffe8d77208a14ed3837~mv2.jpg#originWidth=800&originHeight=797","featured":true,"category":"691f1d67eef7f3399d5b577f","tags":"[\"aee4b7a3-c29b-45d0-8247-6f1bad4b746c\"]"},{"id":"69776864c8e4035bf2b86b8f","title":"Fifty Years of CAPS \u2026 and We\u2019re Looking Ahead to 50 More","slug":"50yearsofcaps","excerpt":"Fifty years ago, a small group of community members asked a brave question:  What if child abuse could be prevented?   That belief sparked what would become CAPS.  What started as a local effort grew into something much bigger, a community-wide commitment to children and families. Over the years, CAPS helped launch Indiana\u2019s first CASA program , opened the state\u2019s first Child Advocacy Center , expanded prevention education, and supported parents before ever reaching a breaking point.  And it...","content":"Fifty years ago, a small group of community members asked a brave question:  What if child abuse could be prevented?  That belief sparked what would become CAPS.  What started as a local effort grew into something much bigger, a community-wide commitment to children and families. Over the years, CAPS helped launch Indiana\u2019s first CASA program, opened the state\u2019s first Child Advocacy Center, expanded prevention education, and supported parents before ever reaching a breaking point.  And it worked.  In 2008, CAPS set a bold goal to reduce child maltreatment in Elkhart County by 30 percent. That goal was achieved not by chance, but through decades of intentional, research-based prevention and partnership.  Today, CAPS continues to evolve, offering innovative programs like Prevent 360\u00b0 and Nurturing Care Groups that meet families where they are and strengthen them early.   Because prevention isn\u2019t a moment, it\u2019s a commitment.  As CAPS enters its 50th year, this milestone isn\u2019t about looking back. It\u2019s about honoring what\u2019s been built and inviting the community into what comes next.  Over the months ahead, we\u2019ll be sharing stories, celebrating milestones, and creating moments that reflect the heart of this work.  Because the future of child safety is something we build together.  Get involved today | Stand Up Against Child Abuse","published":"2026-01-26T13:47:55Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_eb09f96a5b2243e0934ded698355a0a8~mv2.jpg/57b283_eb09f96a5b2243e0934ded698355a0a8~mv2.jpg#originWidth=3124&originHeight=1836","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"6940720d24d4646829ca3d9b","title":"Holiday Safety Tips for Parents and Caregivers","slug":"holiday-safety-tips-for-parents-and-caregivers","excerpt":"Holiday safety tips to keep children safe for parents and caregivers.","content":"The holidays bring busy schedules, gatherings, and lots of visitors. While this season is full of joy, it\u2019s also a good time to be extra mindful about children\u2019s safety and well-being. These simple tips can help create a safe, caring environment for kids during holiday events and celebrations. Respect Consent & Boundaries Children should never feel pressured to hug, kiss, or take photos if they don\u2019t want to. Teach kids that it\u2019s okay to politely say \u201cno,\u201d and model respectful behavior by always asking before touching or hugging. Keep Safety Rules the Same Even during busy gatherings, family safety rules should stay in place. Avoid closed-door situations, make sure children are visible and can be interrupted if needed, and keep an eye on one-on-one time and digital device use. Safe Adults & Code Words Help children identify trusted adults they can go to if they feel uncomfortable or worried. Consider choosing a simple family code word that means \u201cI need help\u201d or \u201cI need a break,\u201d and review safe adults before each event. Secrets vs. Surprises Teach children the difference between secrets and surprises. Secrets are never okay, especially if an adult asks a child to keep something from their parents or caregivers. Surprises, like gifts, are safe because they don\u2019t last forever. Helpful Resources Darkness to Light \u2013 d2l.org Center for Child Protection \u2013 centerforchildprotection.org Monique Burr Foundation \u2013 mbfpreventioneducation.org Mayo Clinic: Holiday Safety Tips \u2013 mayoclinic.org","published":"2025-12-15T05:00:00Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_e0c51c8d38034375bdb62152d43b4567~mv2.png/57b283_e0c51c8d38034375bdb62152d43b4567~mv2.png#originWidth=1280&originHeight=860","featured":false,"category":"686d59126b8307c8dfdcde47","tags":"[\"aee4b7a3-c29b-45d0-8247-6f1bad4b746c\",\"c02eaddd-d5f9-4ebc-8da2-e56496235f12\"]"},{"id":"691f24d102675ac1d2e0c5d0","title":"New CAPS and Maple City Health Care Center Partnership Brings Vital Child Sexual Assault Services to Elkhart County\u00a0","slug":"new-caps-and-maple-city-health-care-center-partnership-brings-vital-child-sexual-assault-services-to","excerpt":"CAPS CEO Rebecca Shetler Fast and SANE Coordinator Elizabeth DIaz at the Maple City Health Center discussing the SANE partnership with CAPS. Children in Elkhart County who experience sexual abuse or assault no longer have to travel miles away to get the specialized medical exams and care they need.   A new partnership between Maple City Health Care Center (MCHCC) and CAPS is bringing Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner services also known as SANE directly to our community.  For years, young...","content":"CAPS CEO Rebecca Shetler Fast and SANE Coordinator Elizabeth DIaz at the Maple City Health Center discussing the SANE partnership with CAPS. Children in Elkhart County who experience sexual abuse or assault no longer have to travel miles away to get the specialized medical exams and care they need.   A new partnership between Maple City Health Care Center (MCHCC) and CAPS is bringing Sexual Assault Nurse Examiner services also known as SANE directly to our community.  For years, young survivors of abuse and assault had to leave the county for a medical forensic exam which is an essential step for healing, safety, and justice. Now, that care is finally available here.  \u201cAnyone who experiences sexual abuse or assault deserves trauma-informed care in a safe, welcoming environment,\u201d says Elizabeth Diaz, SANE Coordinator at MCHCC. \u201cWe\u2019re grateful to partner with CAPS to make that possible for children in our county.\u201d  Through this partnership, children in our community now have access to:  Compassionate, trauma-informed medical exams  Forensic evidence collection  Immediate support and follow-up care  Services delivered by trained SANEs right here in Elkhart County  The SANE program strengthens CAPS\u2019 services and the work of our Child & Family Advocacy Center (CFAC). With medical forensic exams now part of our response, families get consistent, compassionate care from the first report on. And with MCHCC joining CAPS\u2019 Multidisciplinary Team, young survivors have one fully connected group of medical staff and advocates working together to support them.  Together, MCHCC and CAPS are making it easier for young survivors to get the care needed and the dignity they deserve, right here in Elkhart County.","published":"2025-11-20T14:33:40Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg/57b283_7f072fd1e8334f128ac930b88e0ef6f0~mv2.jpg#originWidth=6048&originHeight=4032","featured":false,"category":null,"tags":"[]"},{"id":"691f1d044380ae0a508a57cb","title":"\u201cYou Are Never Alone\u201d\u00a0Why Early Online Safety Education Matters for Kids\u00a0","slug":"you-are-never-alone-why-early-online-safety-education-matters-for-kids","excerpt":"A conversation with National Center for Missing & Exploited Children (NCMEC) trained CAPS educator, Kim Yoder   Kids today are growing up in a digital world that moves fast, sometimes faster than we realize. And while technology opens doors for learnin g and creativity, it also introduces risks earlier than most families expect.  We sat down with CAPS\u2019 NCMEC-trained educator, Kim Yoder , to talk about why early online safety conversations matter, what kids are experiencing, and how parents...","content":"A conversation with National Center for Missing & Exploited Children (NCMEC) trained CAPS educator, Kim Yoder  Kids today are growing up in a digital world that moves fast, sometimes faster than we realize. And while technology opens doors for learnin g and creativity, it also introduces risks earlier than most families expect.  We sat down with CAPS\u2019 NCMEC-trained educator, Kim Yoder, to talk about why early online safety conversations matter, what kids are experiencing, and how parents can build trust without fear.  \u201cMy goal isn\u2019t to scare kids \u2014 it\u2019s to empower them.\u201d  Q: Do young children really need online safety lessons already?  A: Absolutely. Research shows kids as young as seven are hearing new words, getting curious, and searching for answers online, often because they\u2019re afraid to ask a parent. And kids as young as nine are already being targeted by online exploitation. None of this means they\u2019re doing anything wrong. It simply proves how early we need to start giving children safe, accurate information.  Q: What do you hope kids feel when you teach these lessons?  A: Confident. Empowered. Prepared. Technology is a wonderful tool, and I don\u2019t want children to feel afraid of it. I want them to understand what\u2019s safe to share, what to do if something feels wrong, and who their safe adults are \u2014 at home, in school, and in the community.  Q: How does the NCMEC curriculum make complex topics understandable?  A: NCMEC\u2019s Into the Cloud cartoon series does it beautifully. The characters \u2014 Clicky, Webster, and Nettie face situations kids may experience online. We watch short clips, then talk about what happened and why. It turns big concepts like privacy, boundaries, and inappropriate content into simple, kid-friendly decisions.  Q: Is there a moment that made you think, \u201cThis work really matters\u201d?  A: Several. A 4th grader once waited until the end of class to tell me he had received an inappropriate picture and didn\u2019t know what to do. That conversation alone showed me why we must teach these skills early.  Another powerful moment happens almost every time I ask adults or kids if they\u2019ve heard of CyberTipline.org, almost no one has \u2014 even though it\u2019s a lifesaving reporting tool for children facing pressure, extortion, or grooming online. Kids deserve to know where to turn.  Q: Some adults worry these topics might be \u201ctoo much\u201d for little ones. What would you say?  A: If we don\u2019t teach them, the internet will \u2014Early education doesn\u2019t overwhelm kids, it equips them. And it sends a powerful message: You can talk to the safe adults in your life about anything, without fear or punishment.  Q: How can families build on these lessons at home?  A: Use small, everyday moments:  Ask who their friends are \u2014 offline and online.  Play their favorite online games with them.  Show interest instead of judgment.  Keep devices off the punishment list. If kids worry their device will be taken away, they won\u2019t come to us when something is wrong.  Connection always works better than fear.  Q: If every child could remember one message, what would it be?  A: You are never alone. If something happens online: bullying, pressure, inappropriate images, grooming, you can always talk to your safe adults. And anyone can make a report 24/7 at CyberTipline.org, the reporting tool operated by the National Center for Missing & Exploited Children (NCMEC).  Technology isn\u2019t going away, and neither are the challenges that come with it. But when kids learn early, when adults stay open, and when communities work together, we can make the online world a safer place for children. Internet Safety at Home for Parents- Printable Resource","published":"2025-11-20T14:23:29Z","image":"wix:image://v1/57b283_b0a13d9c60a94a10b6effae6f0432edb~mv2.png/57b283_b0a13d9c60a94a10b6effae6f0432edb~mv2.png#originWidth=940&originHeight=788","featured":false,"category":"686d59126b8307c8dfdcde47","tags":"[\"aee4b7a3-c29b-45d0-8247-6f1bad4b746c\",\"ec3032e9-80b9-47c7-8635-da6afc59a80f\",\"ef7a040d-3ddf-4f72-8876-f4f4bba601f5\"]"}];
